        "progress": "PROMISING-LEAD",
        "result": "Three cross-domain mechanisms found in the repo, each converting the DIVERGENT primal envelope into a CONVERGENT dual. RANKED. The unifying observation: each factor chat_T(n_j) is the Fourier coefficient of an indicator of |T| sectors of width 1/7, so |chat_T(n)| = |sin(pi|T|n/7)|/(pi|n|)-type sine/Fejer kernel, BANDLIMITED-like and vanishing at 7|n. That is EXACTLY the setting where Selberg-Beurling majorants and Gaussian/theta lifting beat a naive 1/|n| envelope. The harmonic divergence of MISTAKE-078 comes from replacing a SMOOTH (sine-kernel, cancelling) summand by its crude |.|<=0.6973/|n| absolute value; the smoothness is the discarded resource that all three tools recover.\n\nCANDIDATE 1 (STRONGEST) -- POISSON/THETA LIFT of the signed sum into a positive-definite kernel sum. The signed correction sum_{n in Lambda} K(n) is, by construction, meas(S7(E)) - M7(k) = integral over the torus of (indicator-product - independent-product). Mechanism: write the 1/7-sector indicator's Fourier tail via a Selberg-Beurling MAJORANT/minorant pair S_+/S_- (extremal bandlimited functions of exponential type) for the periodic sector indicator. Then meas(S7(E)) = integral prod (indicator) <= integral prod S_+ where S_+ has FINITELY MANY nonzero Fourier coeffs (compact support in frequency). The product over k coords of a finite-frequency-support majorant has a frequency support contained in a BOX, so the lattice sum sum_{n in Lambda(E)} [prod S_+ coeffs](n) is a sum over Lambda(E) intersect a FIXED box -- automatically FINITE, uniformly in E, with the box size set only by the type of S_+ (a function of the sector geometry 1/7 and k, NOT of E). This is the textbook 'Beurling-Selberg majorant kills the divergent tail' move and it directly gives a uniform-over-E bound: cap reduces to integral prod S_+ which depends only on k. This converts 'bound infinite signed sum' -> 'evaluate a fixed finite frequency-box sum', dissolving the harmonic divergence because S_+ is bandlimited (no 1/|n| tail at all).\n\nCANDIDATE 2 -- BANASZCZYK TRANSFERENCE on the relation lattice Lambda(E). The repo's lattice threads (lrc14-subtorus-relation-lattice-codex, lrc-doubling-law-lattice-proof-s612, tournament-lattice-propagation) all read the orbit through Lambda(E)=ker[e_0..e_{k-1}] (rank k-2). The required-but-never-executed Minkowski/successive-minima count (MISTAKE-078: |K(n)|<=c1^6/(lambda_1...lambda_6)) is precisely a lattice point/theta estimate. Banaszczyk's transference inequality lambda_1(Lambda)*lambda_{k-2}(Lambda*) <= k-2 plus his GAUSSIAN MEASURE bound (the signed sum is exactly a theta-like sum sum_{n in Lambda} f(n) with f Schwartz-dominated by the sine kernel) gives: sum_{n in Lambda, n!=0} |K(n)| <= C(k) * (something)/det-related, CONVERGENT when restricted to support>=6 because the per-coordinate kernel is L^2-summable (sum 1/n^2 = pi^2/6 < infinity) and support>=6 means at least 6 such L^2 factors -> the sum is dominated by a CONVERGENT 6-fold theta product, NOT the divergent 1-fold harmonic sum. KEY FIX of MISTAKE-078: the divergence was computed as a 1-D harmonic sum sum_m 0.6973/|m|; but support>=6 forces a 6-DIMENSIONAL lattice sum where the natural envelope is prod over 6 coords, and a 6-fold lattice sum of prod 1/|n_j| over a rank-(k-2) lattice with bounded covolume CONVERGES once you use the sine-kernel's actual L^2 (1/n^2) decay via Cauchy-Schwarz/theta, not the L^1 (1/n) crude bound. The transference inequality is what makes the convergent dual UNIFORM in E (bounds successive minima of Lambda* by those of Lambda).\n\nCANDIDATE 3 -- LAM-LEUNG VANISHING-SUM / VISIBILITY LAW reduction of the unbounded case. The signed-LRC subgroup-lattice note (s708) proves an EXACT visibility law for signed sine sums at the apex prime: sin(2pi t x/7)=0 iff 7|tx, and silent/vanishing signed sums are governed by the subgroup lattice of Z/7 (here 7 prime -> only trivial lattice). Applied to chat_T(7m)=0 (already noted): the ONLY frequencies n contributing have all coords coprime-to-7-visible. The unbounded->bounded reduction follows from a DILATION + visibility argument: meas(S7) is dilation-invariant (meas(S7)(cE)=meas(S7)(E)), so an unbounded primitive E can be rescaled; the visibility law says only the residues mod 7 of the relation coefficients matter for which K(n) are nonzero, and there are FINITELY MANY residue patterns mod 7 on a rank-(k-2) lattice. So 'no unbounded non-AP exceeds the bounded finite-check max' reduces to: the signed mass K(n) is a function only of the mod-7 visibility class of n, and the worst visibility class is realized by a BOUNDED representative (the AP / near-AP already in the finite check). This is the same coset/coimage quotient that HYP-2640 named the 'signed visible quotient' -- s708 gives it an EXACT subgroup-lattice form via the visibility law.",
        "proof_or_evidence": "Mechanism grounding (not yet a proof): (1) Selberg-Beurling: the engine 04-computation/lrc14_empty_sector_distribution_kps.py computes meas(S7) as integral of products of 1/7-sector indicators; THM-538 already gives chat_T(7m)=0 and per-coord envelope |chat_T(n)|<=0.6973/|n| with 0.6973 = sup|sin(pi n/7)|/(pi|n|)-scale -- i.e. each factor IS a sine kernel, the exact object Beurling-Selberg majorants are built for. A bandlimited majorant S_+ of the sector indicator has finite Fourier support, so prod S_+ has frequency support in a fixed box independent of E; sum over Lambda(E) cap box is finite uniformly. (2) Banaszczyk: MISTAKE-078 itself states the REQUIRED bound is |K(n)|<=c1^6/(lambda_1...lambda_6) over the support-6 lattice -- a successive-minima product -- and that it was 'never executed'. The divergence (partial sum 7.42 at 10^5) is a 1-D L^1 harmonic sum; the true object is a 6-fold sum where sum_n 1/n^2 = pi^2/6 converges, so Cauchy-Schwarz over the 6 nonzero coords gives prod (sum 1/n_j^2)^{1/2} = (pi^2/6)^3 < 17, FINITE. Transference makes this uniform across E by bounding Lambda*'s minima. (3) Lam-Leung/visibility: s708 reflection PROVES the visibility law sin(2pi tx/C)=0 iff C|tx and that signed-sum vanishing is subgroup-lattice-driven; chat_T(7m)=0 is the C=7-prime instance (trivial subgroup lattice => clean), and dilation-invariance of meas(S7) (CLAUDE.md/prompt) + finite mod-7 residue classes reduce unbounded E to bounded representatives. All three are consistent with the verified facts: AP maximizes, correction(AP)=0.253<0.308, worst non-AP is the BOUNDED single-defect near-AP (margin 0.00697), and raw rank is the gate not the meter (HYP-2640).",
        "next_step": "Execute CANDIDATE 1 first (cleanest, fully self-contained): (a) construct the Selberg-Beurling majorant S_+ for the 1/7-periodic indicator of a union of |T| sectors with a chosen type/degree D; (b) verify integral prod_{j=1}^{k} S_+ <= cap_k for k=8,9,10 by a FINITE frequency-box sum over Lambda(E) -- crucially this sum's index set is {n in Lambda(E): ||n||_infty <= D} which is uniformly finite; (c) since prod S_+ >= prod indicator pointwise, meas(S7(E)) <= that finite sum for ALL E, closing the uniform/unbounded case in one stroke. Implement in 04-computation/ extending the existing engine; the only free parameter is D, tune it so the box-sum bound clears cap_9=0.49426 (tightest). If S_+ degree needed is too large to clear cap_9, fall back to CANDIDATE 2: code the 6-fold L^2/theta bound (pi^2/6)^3 * (covolume factor) and pair with Banaszczyk transference for uniformity. Log as HYP-2641 (Beurling-Selberg majorant closes LRC14 wide-spread) and HYP-2642 (6-fold L^2 theta bound corrects the MISTAKE-078 1-D divergence). Cross-link to s708 visibility law and lrc14-subtorus-relation-lattice-codex.",
        "verify": {
          "target": "Three mechanisms (Selberg-Beurling, Banaszczyk L2, Lam-Leung visibility) closing LRC(14) cap bound",
          "verdict": "REFUTED",
          "evidence": "C2 and C3 are category errors shown by exact code; C1 is grounded but unexecuted. C2 claim (pi^2/6)^3<17 false: support-6 envelope over the relation lattice grows (AP 328->880 as L 10->30); cannot factor sum 1/n_j^2 across a rank-6 lattice. C3 claim K(n) residue-only false: coord 1->8 (same mod 7) changes |K| 8x; dilation gives |K|~c^-6 but relation count grows, recreating divergence. C1: main-term inflation harmless (M7_infl(8,3)=0.035<<cap 0.381) but signed truncation does not converge to delta_8=0.303 and a majorant re-incurs C2's divergent envelope; no verified finite-box (E,N)<=cap exists. Kernel validated vs THM-538/THM-503. Exact: AP k=8 meas=481/1470, M7=2880/117649, delta_8=0.30273, cap=2243/5880. Open piece stays HYP-2608(a) wide-spread bound. Files: 04-computation/lrc14_adversarial_{three_mechanisms_verify,C1_majorant_inflation,C2_fast,C2_lattice_envelope,C3_visibility}.py and 05-knowledge/results/*.out."
        }
      }
    ],
    "synthesis": "This independently confirms the critical refutation: `K(1,1,-1,0,0,0,0) = -0.00066 ≠ 0` even though it has support 3 (after zero padding). The \"support-6 floor on full lattice relations\" as literally stated is false — zero padding does not make K vanish, because `chat_T(0) = 1-|T|/7 ≠ 0`. This matches what the third verifier flagged. The floor only holds for the active-coordinate character sum, not for K on real zero-padded relations.\n\nThat's the one independent spot-check I needed. Here is my honest verdict.\n\n---\n\n## VERDICT\n\n### (1) Did any angle CLOSE a piece? — One sub-piece, YES; the endgame, NO.\n\n**CLOSED and verified (single-far-element reduction):** The Weyl/equidistribution angle genuinely closes the *one-far-element* sub-case with explicit constants. The verifier independently reimplemented `meas_S7` in exact `Fraction` arithmetic and reproduced everything:\n- Exact Weyl limit `LIM(B0) = meas(S7)(B0) + (1/7)·P(B0 misses exactly 1 sector)`, O(1/w) convergence.\n- `maxLIM_8 = 289/1470 = 0.19660 < M_8 = 481/1470 = 0.32721` (gap 0.131); `maxLIM_9 = 0.36210 < M_9 = 0.41616` (gap 0.054), both maximized at the consec base.\n- The crude bound `M_{k-1}+1/7` **fails** at k=9 (0.470 > 0.416), so the P1-weighting is genuinely load-bearing — this is a real result, not a restatement.\n\nThis is a real, verified theorem-grade increment. But it is explicitly **STRONG-PARTIAL**: it covers adding *one* far element to a bounded base. The full wide/unbounded case (multiple dissociated far elements) is converted into a precise program (HYP-2641/T889) but **not closed**.\n\n**Everything else is structural insight, not closure.** The Poisson and signed-quotient angles produced genuinely useful *negative* results (below), but did not bound the correction uniformly.\n\n### (2) Most promising route + precise next step\n\n**The far-element contraction (Weyl angle) is the route.** It is the only angle that produced a verified inequality clearing the cap, and the only one whose mechanism (independent sector-fill, each far point fills ≤1 missed sector) generalizes cleanly.\n\n**Precise next step:** Prove the **multi-element tail**. For a bounded base of size `k−r` plus `r` dissociated far elements, generalize `LIM` to `meas(base) + r-fold independent-fill correction`, and verify `max LIM < M_k` persists for r=2,…. The two concrete blockers:\n- (a) Make the multi-frequency Erdős–Turán bound rigorous: the single-frequency discrepancy is `≤ 6/w` per orbit cell (6 sector walls); needs the multi-frequency analog.\n- (b) Route wide *near*-APs through dilation-invariance `meas(cE)=meas(E)` so every primitive non-AP is either bounded (spread `≤ B(k)≈1.25/gap`: B(8)≈10, B(9)≈24 — finite check, done) or contracts below `M_k ≤ cap_k`.\n\nThe signed-quotient angle's factorization `K(n)=D7(n mod 7)/∏ n_j` (verified to 1.7e-19) is the right *bookkeeping* for proving the multi-element tail bound `|Σ_{n_w≠0} K(n)| ≤ C/w` with the antipodal-real weight `Re D7` (`|Re D7|≤0.1431`) — but use it as a **tool inside the Weyl route**, not as a standalone lattice-summation route.\n\n### (3) Genuinely new outside-the-box idea?\n\n**No clean new closer emerged.** Two of the three \"new\" mechanisms were **refuted by exact code**:\n- **Banaszczyk 6-fold L²/theta (C2):** REFUTED. `(π²/6)³ < 17` is false — you cannot factor `Σ 1/n_j²` across a rank-6 relation lattice; the support-6 envelope *grows* (AP: 328→880 as L:10→30). MISTAKE-078's divergence is intrinsic, not a 1-D-vs-6-D artifact.\n- **Lam-Leung visibility / residue-only K (C3):** REFUTED. `K` is not a function of `n mod 7` — coord 1→8 (same residue) changes `|K|` 8×. Dilation gives `|K|~c^{-6}` but the relation count grows, recreating divergence.\n- **Beurling-Selberg majorant (C1):** grounded but **unexecuted**, and likely circular — a bandlimited majorant re-incurs C2's divergent envelope. Worth at most a bounded check, low priority.\n\nThe **Poisson/theta angle's honest payoff is a negative result that should redirect effort:** the convergent dual *is* the existing finite x-cell engine (proven by the elementary pairing `∫₀¹ e^{2πi(n·e)x}dx = 1_{n∈Λ(E)}`), and the symmetric inf-norm box is the *wrong summation order* because the sum is only conditionally convergent. This is valuable — it kills the lattice/box route definitively and confirms the finite-x-cell / far-element plateau is the correct path. But it is **not** a new closer.\n\nI also independently **refuted the R6-density \"ruler\"** claim (odd-AP has higher corr 0.2135 but lower R6 156<546 than near-AP) and confirmed the **THM-538 support-6 floor does not hold on zero-padded full lattice relations** (`K(1,1,-1,0,…)=-0.00066≠0`, reproduced above). Both are real gaps in cited supporting structure — they don't sink the Weyl route but should be corrected in canon.\n\n### (4) Single highest-value next action\n\n**Prove (or numerically certify with explicit constant) the two-far-element correction:** extend `LIM` to a bounded base plus two dissociated far elements as `meas(base) + (1/7)·P(miss exactly 1) + (1/49)·[two-fill cross term]`, and verify `max LIM < M_k` over all bounded bases at k=8,9,10. If that holds with margin (it should — single-element gaps are 0.131/0.054, and each extra far element adds at most ~1/7 of a *conditional* fill), the multi-element induction has its base case and the wide half closes — leaving only the bounded finite check (already done, tight margin 0.023 at k=8 lives there).\n\nEngine to extend: `04-computation/lrc14_weyl_maxlim_adv.py` (exact-Fraction `meas_S7`). Log as HYP-2641/T889.\n\n---\n\n**Bottom line:** LRC(14) is **NOT proved.** One real sub-piece (single-far-element contraction) is verified-closed with explicit constants. The lattice/Poisson/transference routes are now definitively ruled out as shortcuts (this is itself progress — it stops future agents wasting time on MISTAKE-078). The live route is the multi-element Weyl tail; the next concrete action is the two-far-element correction bound. The tight margin (0.023 at k=8) lives entirely in the bounded finite check, which is done."
  }
